</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Mauricio and Casimira, and the children who spread across the interior</h2>
  <p class="dek">Mauricio Lerena married <strong>Casimira Montaño</strong> at San José de Mayo on
    3 February 1837. Their children are why the current family tree points at four different
    departments: <strong>they are not four leads, they are one family moving.</strong></p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">Child</th><th style="width:24%">Record</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {mauricioRows.map((r) => (
          <tr><td><span set:html={emph(r.child)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r.record)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r.grade)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The Trinidad nest</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>At <strong>Trinidad</strong>, the small inland capital of Flores, six or seven Lerena men were
      fathering children between 1871 and 1895 — <strong>Bernabé, Vitalino, Ysaac, Cipriano, Fermín,
      Florencio</strong>. Two of them are now placed: <strong>Bernabé is Alejandro&rsquo;s son and
      Cipriano is Mauricio&rsquo;s, which makes them first cousins.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>The mothers are the other finding, and they are Basque</strong> —
      <em>Beloqui, Echeverry, Pelaez, Gazo, Barragán</em>. A Lerena kindred marrying Basque women in one
      small town speaks to the open question of where the surname comes from; it is evidence about the
      <strong>community they married into</strong>, not about the etymology, and it is recorded as that
      and nothing more.</p>
    <p><strong>And they married each other.</strong> Lerenas and Pelaezes at Trinidad intermarried across
      at least forty years, until by <strong>1885</strong> the church required a
      <strong>dispensation for consanguinity</strong> to let them do it again — twice in the third degree,
      once in the second and third accumulated. <strong>These are not two families who happened to meet.
      They are one intermarried kindred in a small inland town.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What would join the two houses, and what would join either of them to Pablo Armando</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>The houses will not be joined.</strong> That question is closed: Córdoba and Valdemoro
      are five hundred kilometres apart and the act is explicit. What is now open instead is
      <strong>Valdemoro</strong>, which is a finite and searchable place. <strong>Salvador Serena and Ana
      de Cubas were married in Spain</strong>, and that marriage is findable; so is Ramón&rsquo;s own
      baptism, about 1775&ndash;85. The archive has a hard Spanish place-name for this family where an
      hour ago it had none.</p>
    <p>And a second stream opens with it that nobody suspected: <strong>María Josefa González was a
      <em>canaria</em>, from Lanzarote</strong>. The Canary Islands fed their own migration into the River
      Plate, and no part of this archive has ever looked at it.</p>
    <p><strong>And now the thing this page must not be allowed to obscure.</strong>
      <strong>Neither house reaches Pablo Armando.</strong> There is no <em>Juan Carlos Lerena</em> in
      either family, in any generation, in any of the searches that built this page. He was born at
      <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe in Argentina</strong> in 1882, and the Argentine end says the same thing
      from three directions: the cathedral baptism index holds no Lerena for 1881&ndash;83, the 1895
      census holds no Lerena or Lerina household anywhere in Santa Fe province, and his own parents are
      named on exactly one document — <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>his enrolment card</a> — and nowhere
      else on earth.</p>
    <p><strong>So these two families are being built in parallel, and in hope.</strong> The hope is
      reasonable: the surname is rare, the River Plate is one world with a river through the middle of it,
      and <a href={u("/dna/")}>the DNA says the Argentine and Uruguayan hypotheses are genetically the same
      hypothesis</a>. <strong>The hope is not evidence, and nothing here is written as though it were.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-four">The four departments, and what depth means</h2>
  <div class="prose"><p>Coverage is not the same as discovery. What the four departments hold, and what searching them further would and would not buy.</p></div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
