            i === 0 ? <th set:html={emph(v)} /> : <td class="cap" set:html={emph(v)} />
          ))}</tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-household">Sancho&rsquo;s household &mdash; eleven years at Canelones, and four people it owned</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The count of Sancho&rsquo;s children has
      moved twice, and the file that held it was once called complete.</strong> An earlier list held
      <strong>four</strong> children under that name; the reconciled list <strong>supersedes</strong> it and
      names <strong>eight</strong>, born between 1793 and 1815. The padr&oacute;n of 1812 returns
      <strong>six</strong> in the household, which is not a contradiction: two were born after it, and
      Jos&eacute; Loreto, born 1806, is in no tree and no census and was found only because the surname was
      searched as <em>LORENA</em>. The likeliest reading of his absence from the padr&oacute;n is that he
      died before 1812 &mdash; but no burial has been looked for, and none is claimed.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>They lived at Canelones; they did not visit
      it.</strong> Jos&eacute; Loreto&rsquo;s baptism of 11 December 1806 calls Sancho and Mar&iacute;a
      <em>&ldquo;vecinos de esta Villa&rdquo;</em> &mdash; <strong>residents</strong>. That joins
      Agustina&rsquo;s baptism there in 1795, Dominga&rsquo;s in 1804, and Justa Pastora being called
      <em>natural de Canelones</em> in 1831. <strong>The family lived at Canelones for at least eleven
      years, and had moved to Montevideo, calle San Juan N&ordm; 60, by the padr&oacute;n of 1812.</strong>
      The godfather was fetched <em>from</em> Montevideo, which is the other direction.</p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>And the household explains a name.</strong>
      The act of 30 August 1795 at
      Canelones reads <em>&ldquo;bautize, puse el Santo Oleo y Crisma a <strong>AGUSTINA RAMONA, DE TRES
      DIAS NACIDA</strong>, hija legitima de D.<sup>n</sup> Sancho Lerena y de D.<sup>a</sup> Maria
      Fernandez, vecinos de la Villa&rdquo;</em> &mdash; which confirms the birth of 27 August 1795 that
      this register had carried from a tree and a census, and gives her a second forename. Her godfather
      was <strong>Don Fidel Rey, vecino de Montevideo</strong>. Four years later her <strong>younger brother</strong> was
      christened <strong>FIDEL</strong> &mdash; a forename that appears <strong>nowhere else</strong> in
      this family. <strong>This family names children for whoever stands at the font, five times over in the
      acts read here</strong>, so the likeliest source of the Lerena Fidel is that man. It is an
      inference from a pattern and not from a record.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> It also settles nothing about the <strong>other</strong>
      Fidel. A Montevideo death registration of 1889 names <em>&ldquo;Abuelos paternos FIDEL LERENA y
      MARIA RUEDAS&rdquo;</em>, and that plate was opened on 21 September. <strong>Whether that Fidel is
      Sancho&rsquo;s son remains unestablished, and nothing on the plate bears on it.</strong> The tree&rsquo;s
      death of 1822 &mdash; the one thing that would have kept them apart &mdash; is not in the record
      either. The two are still not merged: an unevidenced death is not evidence of survival.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><strong>The padr&oacute;n counted four enslaved people in that house and named none of them.</strong></p>
    <p class="cap">Six weeks later, at the Montevideo Catedral on 26 September 1812, an act names one:
      <em>&ldquo;bautic&eacute; solemnemente a <strong>MARIA MERCEDES</strong>, que naci&oacute; antes de
      ayer, hija de <strong>JUANA</strong>, negra esclava de D.<sup>n</sup> Sancho Lerena y de D.<sup>a</sup>
      Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez: madrina Catalina Mart&iacute;nez.&rdquo;</em></p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>Juana</strong> &mdash; held by Sancho and Mar&iacute;a jointly, as the act says
      and the padr&oacute;n of the same month confirms. No age, no origin, no surname, no later record.
      Her daughter <strong>Mar&iacute;a Mercedes</strong>, born <strong>24 September 1812</strong> and
      baptised at two days old. Nothing is known of what became of her.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>That plate was opened for one reason.</strong> The row had rested on a machine
      transcript, and <strong>a machine is worst at proper nouns &mdash; while this woman&rsquo;s name is
      the only thing any record gives her.</strong> So it was the one that most needed a human to read it.
      It is <strong>JUANA</strong>, in the priest&rsquo;s own hand, film 007713375 image 02628, folio 136.
      This archive carries her name rather than a number.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The same opening carries a trap this archive
      walked past.</strong> The act immediately before hers baptises <strong>another Mar&iacute;a
      Mercedes</strong>, on 25 September 1812 &mdash; <em>&ldquo;hija de <strong>TERESA ABELLEYRA</strong>,
      negra esclava de D.<sup>n</sup> <strong>RAMON ABELLEYRA</strong>&rdquo;</em>. Two enslaved girls of
      the same name, in two households, on consecutive days, on one page. <strong>And this register also
      holds a Teresa enslaved in Sancho&rsquo;s household, from the padr&oacute;n</strong> &mdash; a
      different woman, from a different document. The two must not be run together.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-fs-20sep">What FamilySearch answered on 20 September, and two controls worth more than the answers</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>One of these was found while running a <strong>control</strong> rather than while looking for it:
    the 1877 marriage of Gilberto Lerena and Julia Salvañach may move his parentage from
    <strong>tree</strong> to <strong>documented</strong>. It is not being claimed yet, and the reason is
    in the last row &mdash; <strong>one half of that same index entry is visibly wrong</strong>, which
    is the whole argument for opening the image before trusting the other half.</p>
  </div>
