<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-sancho-alive">When was Sancho last alive? Fourteen records, and only four count</h2>
  <p class="dek">A man can be named in a document for sixty years after he dies. This archive holds
    fourteen records naming Sancho Llerena and asked a narrower question of each of them: does this one
    require him to have been <em>standing there</em>?</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Four do, and the latest is 1812.</strong> He is
      godfather at the Iglesia Matriz in <strong>1789</strong>; a sworn witness at Canelones in
      <strong>1798</strong>, where he took an oath and could not sign it; a <em>padrino</em> at the
      Canelones confirmations in <strong>1804</strong>; and returned in the
      <strong>padr&oacute;n of 1812</strong> at calle San Juan No 60 &mdash; <em>Sancho, andaluz,
      45</em> &mdash; and <strong>a census enumerates the living</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>The other ten name him without requiring him.</strong> Fathers are named on their
      children&rsquo;s baptisms whether present or not. Grandfathers are named on grandchildren&rsquo;s.
      A marriage act names the bride&rsquo;s parents living or dead &mdash; his daughter Justa
      Pastora&rsquo;s of 1830 does <em>not</em> write him <em>difunto</em>, which is suggestive and is
      not evidence. He is named as an ancestor as late as <strong>1850</strong>, sixty-one years after
      that first christening.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>So 1812 is a floor and the trees&rsquo; 1832 is not a fact.</strong> This is
      the same test the whole register is now held to: <a href={u("/who/")}>being named in a later
      record is not evidence of being alive for it</a>, and of 293 people here only twenty are known to
      have died at all.</p>
  </div>
