<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-villademoros">Lerena Villademoros &mdash; a fork the archive refused, and then decided</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On <strong>16 September</strong> a sweep of Sancho&rsquo;s children forward turned up a branch
      this archive had never named: children indexed under the compound <strong>LERENA
      VILLADEMOROS</strong>, and an <strong>Avelino Eduardo Lerena</strong> married to
      <strong>Celedonia Villademoros</strong>.</p>
    <p>It set out two readings and deliberately chose neither. <strong>Either Avelino Lerena
      Fern&aacute;ndez, born 1815, married twice</strong> &mdash; Ambrosia Traibel and then Celedonia
      &mdash; <strong>or Avelino Eduardo was a son of his</strong>, which would make the Villademoros
      children Sancho&rsquo;s great-grandchildren rather than his grandchildren. The note said the
      two-Avelino problem had been suspected before, and left it.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>It is the son.</strong> Two parish acts give
      Avelino Eduardo&rsquo;s <em>own</em> father as <strong>Avelino Serena/Lerena</strong>, with
      <strong>Ambrosia Traibel</strong> named on the record. So he belongs beside Alejandro, Josefa, Ana
      Rosario, Alfredo Justiniano and Mercedes as a child of that marriage, and Celedonia is a
      daughter-in-law.</p>
    <p><strong>The acts, and where they put this family:</strong> <strong>Avelino Pedro Joaqu&iacute;n
      Silvestre</strong>, born December 1865, christened at <strong>Uni&oacute;n, Montevideo on 5 March
      1866</strong>; and <strong>Pedro Gualberto</strong>, born 19 October 1869, christened at the
      <strong>Iglesia del Cord&oacute;n on 15 January 1870</strong>. Celedonia is a daughter of
      <strong>Pedro Villademoros and Ang&eacute;lica M&eacute;ndez</strong>.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>A sentence has been removed from the paragraph above.</strong> It said the
      Uni&oacute;n was <em>&ldquo;the same parish where his cousins the Moratorio girls were baptised
      fourteen years later&rdquo;</em>. On <strong>21 September 2026</strong> that plate was opened and
      the 1880 acts read <em>&ldquo;de esta Bas&iacute;lica menor y Catedral de Montevideo &hellip;
      bautic&eacute; solemnemente <strong>en ella</strong>&rdquo;</em>. <strong>The Moratorio girls
      were baptised in the Catedral, not at the Uni&oacute;n</strong> &mdash; the Uni&oacute;n was the
      <em>index&rsquo;s</em> place field, copied here as though it were testimony. The two christenings
      are not in the same parish and never were.</p>

    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">And then a second Lerena married a second Villademoros</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The same sweep of the Cord&oacute;n turned up
      <strong>Alejandro Joaqu&iacute;n Narciso Lerena</strong>, born 29 October 1872 and christened
      there on <strong>29 May 1873</strong> &mdash; son of <strong>Alejandro Lerena and Elisa
      Villardemoros</strong>. So in one decade, at Montevideo, <strong>two Lerena men married two women
      of that surname</strong>.</p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>Three things tie the two baptisms together.</strong>
      The surname on both mothers. A <strong>Joaqu&iacute;n</strong> standing godfather at each &mdash;
      indexed <em>&ldquo;Joaqu&iacute;n Requena&rdquo;</em> on one and <em>&ldquo;Joaqu&iacute;n Regusa y
      Garc&iacute;a&rdquo;</em> on the other, which is one name and a machine mangling it. And the
      godmother in 1873 is <strong>Ang&eacute;lica Villademoros</strong>, while Celedonia&rsquo;s mother
      is <strong>Ang&eacute;lica M&eacute;ndez</strong> &mdash; a daughter named for her.</p>
    <p><strong>The reading is that two brothers married two sisters</strong>, which is the same shape as
      the two Lerena siblings who married two Cardozo siblings and whose children then petitioned to
      marry each other in 1911. <strong>It is not proved.</strong> The 1873 act does not name
      Alejandro&rsquo;s own parents, so he is <em>not</em> joined here to
      <strong>Alejandro Lerena Traibel</strong> &mdash; Avelino and Ambrosia&rsquo;s eldest, born 1843 or
      1845, who would be twenty-seven or twenty-nine in 1872 and fits perfectly. A surname and an age
      are not an identification. <strong>The test is the marriage act, or any record giving this
      Alejandro a father.</strong></p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>That test was run the same evening and came
      back empty.</strong> <em>Lerena</em> with spouse surname <em>Villademoros</em> in Uruguay returns
      <strong>6,352 records and not one marriage act</strong> &mdash; every hit is a parent entry on
      somebody else&rsquo;s baptism. <strong>The control worked two hours earlier:</strong> the same
      query shape on <em>Lerena</em> with spouse <em>Juanico</em> puts the marriage act of 19 December
      1864 at <strong>rank one</strong>. So the shape finds a marriage when there is one to find.
      <strong>Neither Villademoros marriage is in the indexed record</strong>, and the reading stays an
      inference. What would settle it is the parish marriage books of the Cord&oacute;n and
      Uni&oacute;n, which have not been walked.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>Index-level, and said so.</strong> Both acts are machine-indexed and neither
      image has been opened here. What decides the branch is that <em>two independent entries agree on
      the grandparents</em> &mdash; not that either has been read off the page. And the reason the fork
      existed at all is worth keeping: <strong>three Avelinos in three generations</strong>, which is
      how a grandson gets mistaken for a second marriage.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-san-jose">San Jos&eacute; &mdash; a woman this archive wrote off, and her seven brothers and sisters</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On <strong>14 September</strong> this archive tested <strong>Zelmira Carlota Lerena
      Larriera</strong>, born in the Departamento de San Jos&eacute; on <strong>4 November 1880</strong>,
      and concluded: <em>&ldquo;Tested and placed &mdash; and she is <strong>not</strong> ours.&rdquo;</em></p>
    <p>The same note did something better than its own verdict. It named the one thing that would
      overturn it &mdash; <strong>who her father Alberto Lerena was</strong> &mdash; offered
      <strong>Alberto Octavio Lerena Lenguas</strong> as a candidate, and then refused to draw the line:
      <em>&ldquo;Nothing joins them but a forename and an age, which is precisely the evidence that
      produced the two Arturos.&rdquo;</em></p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>She is ours, and the candidate was the wrong
      man.</strong> Alberto Lerena&rsquo;s own Montevideo death registration of <strong>2 March 1921</strong>,
      certificate 246, names his wife <strong>Mar&iacute;a In&eacute;s Larriera</strong> and his parents
      <strong>Avelino Lerena and Ambrosia Treibel</strong>. So her father is <strong>Pedro Alberto
      Ysabelino Lerena Traibel</strong>, baptised at the Aguada on 21 November 1852 &mdash; and Zelmira is
      a <strong>great-granddaughter of Sancho</strong>, by the other grandfather.</p>
    <p><strong>And she is one of eight.</strong> Asking for Alberto and Mar&iacute;a In&eacute;s as a
      <em>pair</em> returns sixteen Uruguayan records and a whole household, born in the Departamento de
      San Jos&eacute; between 1879 and 1898.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:20rem">Child</th><th class="m" style="width:8rem">Born</th>
        <th style="width:12rem">Where</th><th>Record</th><th class="m" style="width:7rem">Tier</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {sanjose.map((r) => {
          const c = Object.values(r);
          return (
            <tr>
              <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
              <td class="m" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
              <td class="cap">{c[2]}</td>
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
              <td class="m">{c[4]}</td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>Three of the eight carry names out of the
      household at the Aguada</strong> &mdash; <strong>Mar&iacute;a Adelina</strong> for his sister,
      <strong>Carlos Ambrosio</strong> for his brother, and <strong>&Aacute;ngel Miguel Avelino</strong>
      for his father. That is corroboration and it is not evidence. These are common names, and this
      archive has been burned by exactly this reasoning before. <strong>The death registration is the
      evidence; the names are why one believes it.</strong></p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The weak link was named and then closed the
      same day.</strong> That 1921 registration had been read at index level only, so the image was
      opened &mdash; <strong>film 008165205, image 01604</strong>. It reads:
      <em>&ldquo;Que el finado era hijo de <strong>Avelino Lerena</strong>, fallecido, y de
      <strong>Ambrosia Treibel</strong>, fallecida.&rdquo;</em> The chain now rests on an image at
      every step that matters.</p>
    <p><strong>And the act does something the index could not.</strong> It counts them:
      <em>&ldquo;era casado con Mar&iacute;a In&eacute;s Larriera, de cuya uni&oacute;n quedan
      <strong>nueve hijos</strong> llamados: Mar&iacute;a Adelina, Zelmira, <strong>Eleiza</strong>,
      Alberto, Carlos, Mar&iacute;a In&eacute;s, Mar&iacute;a Consuelo, Mar&iacute;a Antonieta y
      Miguel Avelino.&rdquo;</em> Eight of those nine are the eight above. <strong>The third is a
      daughter no index here has matched</strong>, and her name is not securely read &mdash; it is
      <em>Eleiza</em> or <em>Eloiza</em>, read at three times magnification, and this archive cannot
      choose between them.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>Which is also the point about eight index entries.</strong> They look like
      eight independent confirmations. They are one indexing project reading one set of registers.
      This single act, written from the declarations of two men who knew the household, is a different
      occasion and a different mouth &mdash; and it both confirms the eight and corrects the count.</p>
    <p class="cap">The act adds what no index carried: he was <strong>born at Montevideo</strong>, not
      in San Jos&eacute; where his children were; he was a <strong>rentista</strong>; he died at six in
      the morning at the calle Juan Paullier, of a carcinoma certified by a Dr. Lussich; and the two
      declarants were neighbours, not kin &mdash; which is why the act makes him sixty-nine fifteen
      days before he would have been.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>What it moves, beyond one woman&rsquo;s parents.</strong> This register has
      carried a San Jos&eacute; de Mayo cluster since its first fortnight &mdash; Echeverry, an Alejandro
      Lerena, a B&aacute;rbara Lerena, and the Mauricio Lerena who married Casimira Monta&ntilde;o there
      in 1837 &mdash; all unattached, in a department the Montevideo trunk never reached. <strong>This is
      the first documented Lerena of that trunk living in San Jos&eacute;</strong>, from 1879 to 1898. It
      joins nothing. It puts a known man in the room where that cluster lives, which is a smaller thing
      and a reason to look again.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-gonzalez-lerena">González Lerena &mdash; a branch no sweep had ever counted</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This one exists because of a mistake. A full-text search for <em>Juan Carlos Lerena</em> matched
    <strong>across</strong> the compound surname <em>Juan Carlos González Lerena</em> and was written
    off as a false positive &mdash; and the variant that produced it turned out to hide a whole family
    of <strong>fifty-two records</strong> that no sweep here had ever counted.</p>
    <p>At the head of it is <strong>Estefanía Lerena</strong>, who married Servando González, with at
    least six children across San José, Montevideo and Colonia between 1854 and 1931.
    <strong>She is attached to nothing</strong> and is recorded unplaced.</p>
  </div>
