<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-dominga-1836">Dominga, in a household at last</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Sancho's youngest child was <strong>Dominga, aged seven in the 1812 padrón</strong>, and this
    archive has <strong>never followed her past that page</strong>. The seven-spelling sweep turned up a
    <strong>Dominga</strong> of the right generation in the <strong>1836 census of Montevideo</strong>
    &mdash; and the page puts her in a household.</p>
    <p>She is the wife of <strong>D. Antonio R. Carballo</strong>, a <em>hacendado</em>; both are entered
    <em>casado</em>; and the two unmarried daughters of the house are <strong>Lucinda</strong> and
    <strong>Dominga Carballo</strong> &mdash; <strong>a daughter named for her mother</strong>, which is
    the naming discipline this family keeps.</p>
    <p><strong>She was never a candidate, and saying so was my error.</strong> This archive proved her
    on <strong>14 September</strong>: <strong>Dominga Josefa Llerena</strong>, born 13 May 1804 at
    Canelones, daughter of Sancho, married <strong>Capitán Antonio Ruiz de Carvallo</strong> &mdash; the
    Catedral act of 12 May 1827 names her outright as <em>Doña Dominga Llerena de Carvallo</em>, and the
    <strong>Lucinda</strong> of this census is the <strong>Lucinda Gregoria</strong> baptised that very
    day. I added a duplicate of her to the register before checking; it has been removed and its
    evidence folded into the woman already here.</p>
    <p>What the census and the 1862 act <em>do</em> add is real: the household, his trade, a second
    daughter named for her mother &mdash; and <strong>Josefa Carballo, born in Rio de Janeiro</strong>.
    <strong>Brazil is the one genuinely new country in any of it.</strong></p>
  </div>
