</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-film-map">The reel, mapped by brightness</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Everything above came off <strong>six images of a reel of three thousand two hundred</strong>.
    The reel is the Archivo General de la Nación's <em>Padrones de Montevideo y Extramuros</em>, and
    until today nobody here knew where one volume ended and the next began.</p>
    <p>It is mapped now, and not by looking. Each thumbnail was drawn to a small canvas and its mean
    brightness measured: <strong>filming target boards read 16&ndash;30, ordinary pages 140&ndash;156</strong>,
    with nothing in between. <strong>Eighteen dark-frame clusters, 1,604 thumbnails, three calls, no session and no screenshots</strong> &mdash; and then every one of them checked. The reel holds <strong>fifteen volumes</strong>, each opening with a title board and closing with a <em>fin del ítem</em> card. Twelve further dark frames turned out to be <strong>book covers, spines and blanks</strong>, which is why a dark frame could not simply be called a boundary.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:15rem">What</th><th style="width:20rem">Detail</th>
        <th>Why it is useful</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {filmmap.map((r) => { const c = Object.values(r); return (
          <tr>
            <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
          </tr>); })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-alejandros">Four men called Alejandro &mdash; and the one whose parents are now known</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-bottom:20px">
    <p><strong>Answered on 27 September 2026.</strong> <strong>Alejandro M. Lerena &mdash; the father on
      the Argentine army enrolment cards of Caj&oacute;n 985 &mdash; is a son of AVELINO LERENA and
      AMBROSIA TRAIBEL.</strong> This archive had called his parentage <em>&ldquo;the whole
      obstacle&rdquo;</em> since 14 September.</p>
    <p class="cap">The Cord&oacute;n christening of his daughter <strong>Jacinta Mercedes
      Gregoria</strong>, 26 December 1877, read off <strong>film 007713388 image 02455</strong>:
      <em>&ldquo;hija leg&iacute;tima de D. <strong>ALEJANDRO M. LERENA</strong> y de D.&ordf; ELISA
      [Villademoros] <strong>(hoy finada)</strong>, orientales. <strong>Abuelos paternos D. AVELINO y
      D.&ordf; AMBROSIA TRAIBEL</strong>, maternos D. Pedro y D.&ordf; Ang&eacute;lica
      M&eacute;ndez.&rdquo;</em></p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>So he is a Lerena Traibel</strong> &mdash; a grandson of Sancho Llerena, and a
      brother of Josefa, Alberto, Alfredo Justiniano and Ana Rosario. The Argentine cards and the
      Montevideo house are one family.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom:20px">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The route matters more than the answer.</strong>
      It was not found by looking for his parents, which had been tried for thirteen days. It was found
      by looking for <strong>his first wife</strong> &mdash; he is <em>viudo</em> on both acts of 1886 and
      1888, so one had to exist. And the record was indexed under
      <strong>&ldquo;Alejandro M. <em>Serena</em>&rdquo;</strong>, with the grandmother as
      <strong>&ldquo;Ambrosia <em>Trecibel</em>&rdquo;</strong>. <strong>A search under LERENA would never
      have returned it.</strong> The plate says LERENA and TRAIBEL in the priest&rsquo;s hand.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>And the widowhood is dated.</strong> Elisa is
      <em>hoy finada</em> &mdash; dead that day &mdash; on 26 December 1877, and her daughter was born
      <strong>19 September 1876</strong>. <strong>She died inside those fifteen months</strong>, nine
      years before the act that calls him a widower.</p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>What is left is a merge, not a question.</strong>
      This register still holds <strong>Alejandro Lerena Traibel</strong>, baptised at La Mauricia on
      7 January 1846 &mdash; a son of the same couple, with the same forename, whose birth the act puts at
      29 May 1843 or 1845 against the <strong>1844</strong> his own stated ages give. Nothing separates
      them but the possibility of two sons sharing a name, which happens when the first dies, and nothing
      here shows the 1846 infant died. <strong>They are almost certainly one man, and they are not yet
      merged</strong> &mdash; because this archive has merged on less and been wrong.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The eldest known child of <strong>Avelino Lerena and Ambrosia Traibel</strong> was baptised
      <strong>Alejandro</strong> at La Mauricia on 7 January 1846. Somewhere in Argentina, on the
      enrolment cards of <a href={u("/argentina/")}>army drawer Caj&oacute;n 985</a>, a father is named
      <strong>Alejandro M. Lerena</strong>. This archive has asked since 14 September whether they are
      one man, and has always answered <em>inconclusive</em>.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The answer stayed inconclusive for a reason that
      turned out to be false.</strong> The working log held the two apart on a <strong>six-year
      gap</strong> &mdash; a baptism pointing at 1843 or 1845, against a stated <em>&ldquo;born Uruguay
      <strong>1839</strong>&rdquo;</em>. That 1839 was graded <strong>PRIMARY (indexed)</strong> and
      sourced to the Capital Federal parish records. <strong>On 22 September all four of those records
      were opened, and every one of them leaves the father&rsquo;s age blank</strong> &mdash; while
      giving the mother&rsquo;s. The 1839 came from a <strong>family tree</strong>, and was re-graded as
      a record on the way in. It was also printed on this page, in the descent above, as a plain fact.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Then he told us himself, twice.</strong> Two
      Montevideo birth acts of his own children were read off the plate &mdash; <strong>film 004596830,
      images 01806 and 02145</strong>. He stood in front of the officer and gave his age:
      <em>&ldquo;don Alejandro M. Lerena, de <strong>cuarenta y dos a&ntilde;os de edad</strong>, viudo,
      escribano, oriental, domiciliado en la calle San Jos&eacute; N 17&rdquo;</em> on 6 November 1886,
      and <em>&ldquo;don Alejandro M. Lerena <strong>de 43 a&ntilde;os de edad</strong> de estado viudo
      de nacionalidad Oriental&rdquo;</em> on 16 March 1888. Sixteen months apart, and consistent with
      each other. <strong>That puts his birth between 17 March and 6 November 1844.</strong></p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table style="min-width:78rem">
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:13rem">Who</th><th style="width:17rem">Born</th>
        <th style="width:9rem">Wife</th><th style="width:19rem">Children and records</th>
        <th style="width:20rem">His parents</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {alejandros.map((r) => { const c = Object.values(r); return (
          <tr>
            <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[4])} />
          </tr>); })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>The gap is now one year, and it still cannot
      choose.</strong> Against a birth of 29 May 1843 he should have said 43 and 44; against 29 May 1845,
      41 and 42. He said <strong>42 and 43</strong>. The stated ages miss both candidate dates by exactly
      one year, <strong>in opposite directions</strong> &mdash; they sit precisely between them. A man
      consistently one year off a baptism act is the ordinary case and this archive sees it weekly. A man
      five years off, twice, is a different man or a bad number. <strong>So 1839 is gone, the mid-1840s
      are in, and that is the first thing that has ever pointed these two rows at each other.</strong>
      It is circumstantial, and they remain separate rows.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The form asked this archive&rsquo;s exact
      question and nobody wrote the answer on it.</strong> The 1888 act is a printed sheet with a ruled
      line reading <em>&ldquo;Que es nieta por l&iacute;nea paterna de&nbsp;______&rdquo;</em>. It is
      <strong>blank</strong>, and so is the maternal one. That is common on these forms and is
      <strong>not</strong> evidence that he could not have answered.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>What the two acts did give is a route.</strong>
      He is <strong>viudo</strong> on both &mdash; a widower at forty-two, while Julia Bassani is
      twenty-two and <em>soltera</em>, living at a different address, where both children were born. So
      there is an earlier marriage and an earlier death in Montevideo, <strong>and a marriage act names
      the groom&rsquo;s parents</strong>. He is also an <strong>escribano</strong>, a notary, and
      notaries leave protocolos filed under their own names. Those are the two things left to try.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>And one row on this site was two men.</strong>
      The register carried a bare <em>&ldquo;Hector Lerena&rdquo;</em> of Buenos Aires who married
      <strong>Amalia Pacheco</strong>. His marriage act of 25 February 1911 at San Carlos was opened:
      <em>&ldquo;hijo leg&iacute;timo de D. <strong>Alejandro M&aacute;ximo</strong> &hellip; y de
      do&ntilde;a <strong>Julia Ba[ssani]</strong>, natural de Italia &hellip; con do&ntilde;a
      <strong>Amalia Pa[checo]</strong>&rdquo;</em>, the groom <em>&ldquo;de la Rep. Oriental, de veinte
      y cuatro&rdquo;</em>. The wife matches, the parents match, and twenty-four in February 1911 fits a
      birth on <strong>29 October 1886 at Montevideo</strong> exactly. <strong>They are one man, and the
      two rows are now one.</strong> The index had him born 1887 at Almagro, Buenos Aires; the act says
      <em>Oriental</em>, and the indexer had defaulted the birthplace to the place of the wedding.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>That act also spells out a letter.</strong> On
      the two Montevideo acts he is only ever <strong>Alejandro M. Lerena</strong>, and on the
      enrolment cards of Caj&oacute;n 985 he is only ever <strong>Alejandro M. Lerena</strong>. In the
      priest&rsquo;s hand at San Carlos in 1911 the initial is written out: <strong>M&aacute;ximo</strong>.
      <strong>So the <em>expansion</em> of that M. is now on a plate rather than in a tree, and the
      Alejandro M. Lerena of Caj&oacute;n 985 carries the same one.</strong> It is worth being exact about
      what that does and does not do: it joins the <em>cards</em> to the <em>Montevideo acts</em>, which
      were never in doubt &mdash; the same wife and the same children stand on both. It does not join
      either of them to Avelino&rsquo;s son.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>And on 27 September a fourth Alejandro walked
      into the middle of it.</strong> Hunting the first marriage &mdash; he is a <em>widower</em> in
      1886, so one must exist &mdash; this archive opened a Montevideo christening the index dates to
      <strong>1919</strong>. <strong>The act is 1872.</strong> The 1919 is the certificate:
      <em>&ldquo;expido la presente &hellip; en Montevideo a veintiocho de Septiembre de mil novecientos
      diez y nueve&rdquo;</em>, signed Ram&oacute;n Howard at the Bas&iacute;lica Metropolitana.
      <strong>Forty-seven years between the two dates the index could have taken</strong>, and it took
      the wrong one.</p>
    <p>What the act says matters more. A daughter of <strong>Avelino Lerena</strong> &mdash; called
      <strong><em>argentino</em></strong>, with his wife <strong>Celedonia Villademoros</strong>
      <em>argentina</em> &mdash; is baptised at the Iglesia Matriz on 29 September 1872, and her
      paternal grandparents are written out: <em>&ldquo;Abuelos paternos <strong>Avelino y Ambrosia
      Traibel</strong>&rdquo;</em>. That puts Avelino Eduardo&rsquo;s descent on a parish act instead of
      an index, adds a third child &mdash; <strong>Ang&eacute;lica Mar&iacute;a, born 8 April 1872</strong>
      &mdash; and tells us this branch of the Lerena Traibel was <strong>Argentine-born</strong>.</p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>And it names the godparents:</strong>
      <em>&ldquo;Fueron padrinos <strong>ALEJANDRO M. LERENA y ELISA VILLADEMOROS</strong>, a quienes
      instruy&oacute;.&rdquo;</em> This register holds a separate <strong>Alejandro Lerena who married
      Elisa Villardemoros</strong> and had a son christened at the Cord&oacute;n in May 1873. On
      22 September this archive declared those two men <strong>different</strong>, and gave as its
      reason that one of them was <em>a widower</em>. <strong>That is backwards.</strong> A widower in
      1886 is a man whose earlier wife has died &mdash; the strongest link in the evidence had been
      written down as the objection to it. <strong>The M. is carried by no other Alejandro here.</strong></p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>And a third record pairs him with Elisa, in
      Argentina.</strong> Their son <strong>Alejandro Joaqu&iacute;n</strong> &mdash; christened at the
      Cord&oacute;n in May 1873 &mdash; married <strong>Amalia Casas</strong> at the Inmaculada
      Concepci&oacute;n, <strong>Bell Ville, C&oacute;rdoba</strong>, in 1907, and that act names his
      parents <em>&ldquo;Alejandro M Lerena&rdquo;</em> and <em>&ldquo;Elisa Villademoras&rdquo;</em>.
      A Montevideo font in 1872, a Cord&oacute;n christening in 1877 and a C&oacute;rdoba marriage in
      1907 all carry the same couple and the same initial. <strong>That C&oacute;rdoba plate was then opened, and it gives
      what the index could not.</strong> The act of <strong>20 June 1907</strong> at the Parroquia de
      <strong>San Jer&oacute;nimo</strong> reads: <em>&ldquo;Alejandro Joaqu&iacute;n Lerena, de treinta
      y cuatro a&ntilde;os de edad, <strong>natural de MONTEVIDEO, Rep&uacute;blica Oriental</strong>
      &hellip; hijo leg&iacute;timo de <strong>Alejandro M. [Lerena] y Elisa Villademoras
      (FINADOS)</strong>&rdquo;</em>.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>&ldquo;Finados&rdquo; is the first evidence from
      any record that Alejandro M. Lerena was dead.</strong> He appeared in person at Montevideo in March
      1888 to register Emilia Alberta; by June 1907 he was gone. <strong>His death falls in that
      bracket</strong> &mdash; and the family tree&rsquo;s &ldquo;1903&rdquo;, which this archive has
      never been able to test, now at least sits inside a ceiling a record provides.</p>
    <p>The plate corrects the index three times over. The index gives only <em>1907</em>; the act says
      <strong>20 June</strong>. The index names the parish <em>Inmaculada Concepci&oacute;n, Bell
      Ville</em>; the act says <strong>San Jer&oacute;nimo</strong>. And the index reads the surname
      <strong><em>Serena</em></strong> &mdash; where the plate has a plain <strong>L</strong>, sitting on
      the same opening as the scribe&rsquo;s own <em>S</em> in <em>San Jer&oacute;nimo</em>.
      <strong>That is the sixth S-for-L this archive has caught, and the second in one day.</strong></p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>Two details on that act are not explained here.</strong>
      The printed word <em>Iglesia</em> is struck through and <em>casa particular de la novia</em> written
      over it, and the banns were <strong>dispensed</strong>. A wedding in the bride&rsquo;s own house
      with the proclamations waived is what haste or illness looks like on a parish form. The bride is
      <strong>Amalia Casas</strong>, daughter of Cornelio Casas and Raimunda Pereda. <strong>So the son of the first marriage
      crossed too</strong> &mdash; his half-brothers and half-sisters by Julia Bassani went to Misiones
      and Buenos Aires; he went to C&oacute;rdoba. <span class="chip lore">Index only</span></p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>They are still not merged.</strong> An initial
      and a wife is not proof, and this archive has merged on less and been wrong. The Cord&oacute;n
      plate of 1873 was opened the same day in the hope of grandparents and
      <strong>gives none</strong> &mdash; that parish&rsquo;s form does not recite them &mdash; though it
      does call him <em>oriental</em>, as the 1886 and 1888 acts do. <strong>Two records would settle
      it:</strong> the marriage of Alejandro Lerena and Elisa Villademoros, before October 1872, or her
      death, between 1873 and 1886. Either names him; <strong>and a marriage act names his
      parents.</strong></p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>Which makes the family&rsquo;s road visible.</strong>
      H&eacute;ctor born Montevideo 1886, Emilia Alberta Montevideo 1888, Roberto Juan in
      <strong>Misiones</strong> 1891, R&oacute;mer Rosario in Buenos Aires 1895, then Elisa Cornelia
      1898, Carmen Romualda 1901 and Mar&iacute;a Luisa 1903 &mdash; the last three baptised together
      late, in 1905, 1907 and 1908. <strong>They left Montevideo between 1888 and 1891 and crossed the
      country before settling in the capital.</strong> Julia is <em>italiana</em> in 1886 and
      <em>austriaca</em> in 1888, aged 22 and then 24 &mdash; both written in front of her, and this
      archive records both rather than picking.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-trinidad-sweep">Trinidad, Flores &mdash; the dispensation was granted</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> On <strong>27 September 2026</strong> this archive
      opened all thirty-three findmypast transcripts for Uruguayan Lerena baptisms, which gave twenty
      children across fourteen households. <strong>A single FamilySearch search on the same place then
      returned a generation findmypast does not hold at all</strong> &mdash; the children of those
      children. <strong>One index is not another index, and neither is the parish book.</strong></p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>And it closed a story this archive has carried
      open since it found the diagrams.</strong> In 1911 the Cura Vicario of Montevideo drew two kinship
      charts for <strong>Custodio Lerena</strong> and <strong>Ema Cardozo</strong>, double first cousins,
      seeking dispensation to marry. Whether it was granted has never been known here.
      <strong>Their daughter Violeta Blanca was born on 29 October 1915</strong> and christened at
      Trinidad the following September. <strong>It was granted, and they married.</strong></p>
  </div>
